course: dictionary
lesson: 710
title: Barefoot in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Barefoot means not wearing any shoes or socks on your feet. You can often work it out from the word itself: bare means with nothing covering it, and foot is your foot.
explain: «Lucas walks barefoot across the wet floor every week and ends up with verrucas on three toes.» The clue is that his feet touch the floor with nothing between them. Then the sentence adds: «a pair of flip-flops would have kept his feet off the floor». Shoes would protect him, so being barefoot means having no shoes.
explain: Barefoot can be an adjective, as in «a barefoot runner», or an adverb, as in «walks barefoot». Barefooted means the same thing. To check your meaning, swap in "without shoes" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Barefoot means not wearing any shoes or socks on your feet.
fact: Barefoot can be an adjective («a barefoot runner») or an adverb («walks barefoot»). Barefooted means the same.
fact: Going barefoot outside can lead to cuts from glass, stings from bees in clover, and burns from hot sand or pavement, so wear shoes in parks and playgrounds.
fact: In PE, gymnastics and yoga are often done barefoot.
fact: «Rubbing makes skin grow thicker into calluses, which protect it.» Barefoot runners grow thick soles that protect like shoes.
exercise: «Lucas walks barefoot across the wet floor every week.» What does barefoot mean here? | *Without any shoes or socks on his feet | With wet socks on his feet | Running very fast across the floor | In a pair of flip-flops
exercise: Why should you wear shoes in a park? | Because parks do not allow children to run | *Because there may be broken glass or bees in the clover | Because shoes make you run faster | Because grass is always too hot to stand on
exercise: «Rubbing makes skin grow thicker into calluses, which protect it.» What do calluses do? | *Protect the skin | Wash the feet | Make the skin cold | Hurt the skin
test: Which activity is often done barefoot in PE? | *Gymnastics | Cross-country skiing | Football with boots on | Cycling to school
test: «Lucas walks barefoot across the wet floor every week and ends up with verrucas on three toes.» What does the sentence suggest? | Wet floors make shoes dry faster | Feet grow wings when they get wet | His toes get longer every week | *Going without shoes can leave his toes with verrucas
exam: What is a good way to check what barefoot means in a sentence? | Guess the first thing that comes to mind and never check | Count the letters in the word | *Swap in "without shoes" and see if the sentence still makes sense | Skip the word and never come back to it
